---
# FLUX, WATCHED BECAUSE IT IS NOW THE DEPLOY PATH.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT THESE RULES ARE ACTUALLY FOR
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A deploy used to be a CI job, and a broken deploy was a red job in a list somebody reads. It is
# now four controllers agreeing: source-controller fetches the commit, image-reflector-controller
# scans the registry, image-automation-controller commits the winning tag, kustomize-controller
# applies it. Any of them can stop WITHOUT ANYTHING GOING RED, because a custom resource turning
# `Ready=False` is not an event that reaches a human.
#
# The failure that produces is not an outage. It is the cluster serving the last image it happened
# to receive, forever, while every CI run stays green -- the same silent staleness that caused the
# 2026-09-03 outage and put Flux here to begin with. Replacing a loud failure with a quiet one
# would have been a downgrade, so these rules exist as a condition of retiring `kubectl set image`
# rather than as a follow-up to it.
#
# ⚠️ THIS FILE NO LONGER WATCHES WHETHER A RECONCILE SUCCEEDS. It was written around
# `gotk_reconcile_condition`, whose 0/1-per-(kind,name,type,status) shape is why the rules said
# `== 1` rather than testing for the series -- an expression that omits the comparison fires the
# moment Flux reports anything at all. That care was correct and is now moot: this fleet's Flux has
# never published that gauge, nor `gotk_suspend_status`, so the three rules built on them were
# permanently green. They are gone, replaced by one that watches reconciles CEASING, which is what
# the remaining metrics can actually say -- for the objects that reconcile on a CLOCK. ImagePolicy
# does not, and is excluded; see the note on the rule. Reconciliation FAILURE and a SUSPENDED object are
# watched by the `flux-objects` group at the bottom, from `flux:resource_info` -- recorded from the
# `gotk_<kind>_info` series kube-state-metrics publishes only once it is given a
# `--custom-resource-state-config` for Flux's custom resources. That config lives in the gitops repository; if it is ever missing, the group's
# `absent()` companion fires and says so.
groups:
  - name: flux
    rules:
      # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # THREE ALERTS STOOD HERE THAT COULD NEVER FIRE, AND THAT IS WORTH RECORDING RATHER THAN
      # QUIETLY REPLACING
      # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      #
      # `FluxReconciliationFailing`, `FluxDeployPathStalled` and `FluxSuspended` selected on
      # `gotk_reconcile_condition` and `gotk_suspend_status`. Neither has EVER had a series on this
      # fleet -- `count(max_over_time(...[30d]))` returns 0 for both, against 22 for the duration
      # counter beside them. Newer flux controllers dropped those gauges; the only `gotk_*` families
      # published here are `gotk_reconcile_duration_seconds_{bucket,count,sum}` and `gotk_token_*`.
      # So all three were permanently green for the same reason a deleted alert is green, and the
      # deploy path they guarded was unwatched for as long as they existed.
      #
      # ⚠️ "NO SERIES RIGHT NOW" IS NOT THE TEST, and using it as one produces false accusations.
      # `kube_pod_container_status_waiting_reason` and `nixos_auto_apply_blocked_reason` both read
      # zero at an arbitrary instant and both are healthy: they are published only while a pod is
      # waiting or a host is blocked, so absence is the good state. The question is whether a metric
      # has EVER had a series, over a window longer than the condition it reports. Asking it the
      # other way nearly cost K3sPodCrashLooping, which is fine, a needless rewrite.
      #
      # WHAT REPLACES THEM IS WEAKER, AND SAYING SO IS THE POINT. A stopped reconciler and a FAILING
      # one are different failures, and only the first is detectable from what Flux emits here: the
      # duration histogram counts reconciles, so it can say they have ceased, but not that one came
      # back NotReady. A Kustomization that reconciles briskly and fails every time still increments
      # this counter and stays silent here.
      #
      # THE FAILURE AND SUSPEND SIGNALS COME BACK in the `flux-objects` group below, read from the
      # objects' own status through kube-state-metrics rather than from gauges the controllers no
      # longer publish.
      #
      # ONE HOUR AGAINST A MEASURED CADENCE, not a round number: the busiest objects reconcile 12-14
      # times an hour (ImageUpdateAutomation/movies 14.1, GitRepository/movies 12.1), so an hour at
      # zero is far outside normal for anything that reconciles at all. Per-series, so it names the
      # object that stopped rather than the fleet.
      #
      # ⚠️ `kind!="ImagePolicy"` BECAUSE THAT KIND HAS NO CADENCE TO MEASURE AGAINST, and this rule
      # paged on it before anyone read the spec. An ImagePolicy takes no `interval`:
      # image-reflector-controller evaluates it only when the ImageRepository it points at completes
      # a scan that CHANGED the tag set, so its counter advances once per image build. Seven days on
      # this fleet, `increase(...[7d])`: every other object 610-1197, ImagePolicy/movies-web 83 and
      # ImagePolicy/movies-worker 92 -- which is the merge count, not a cadence. The condition was
      # consequently true 46% and 43% of the time respectively, on a deploy path that was healthy
      # throughout: on 2026-09-08 it fired for movies-web while the policy held
      # main-20260907234535-fc94b3e, the tag for the then-current HEAD, and every Kustomization read
      # Applied.
      #
      # THE EXCLUSION GIVES UP NOTHING, which is why it is the fix rather than a silence. The
      # ImageRepository beside it runs the registry scan that FEEDS the policy, on a 5m `interval`,
      # under the same image-reflector-controller and scraped from the same target -- so a wedged
      # reflector flatlines ImageRepository/movies-web (1141 reconciles over those same 7 days) and
      # still pages here, one series later in the same chain. The only lost signal is a policy
      # wedged during a week with no merges, which this counter could not tell from a quiet week
      # anyway.
      - alert: FluxReconciliationStopped
        expr: increase(gotk_reconcile_duration_seconds_count{kind!="ImagePolicy"}[1h]) == 0
        # The `increase` window is already an hour; this only confirms it is not one odd sample at a
        # scrape boundary.
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "flux {{ $labels.kind }}/{{ $labels.name }} has not reconciled for an hour"
          description: >-
            `gotk_reconcile_duration_seconds_count` for this object has not moved in an hour, while
            the busiest objects on this cluster reconcile 12-14 times an hour. Either its controller
            is wedged, the object is suspended, or its source is unreachable. This is the DEPLOY
            PATH: a stopped Kustomization means merged changes stop reaching pods, silently, with CI
            green and the image built -- the state that hid a read-model fix for hours on
            2026-09-07. Check `flux get all -A` on monitoring-1 and the controller's own output in
            the log store (`namespace:flux-system`). This alert does NOT watch reconciliation
            FAILURE: an object that reconciles and fails every time keeps this counter moving and
            stays silent here. ImagePolicy is EXCLUDED: it has no interval and advances only when
            an image is built, so an hour of silence is its normal state -- a wedged
            image-reflector-controller is caught by its ImageRepository instead.


      # THE EXPORTER ITSELF. Without this the reconcile rule above goes quiet exactly when the
      # thing it watches dies hardest, because a controller that is down publishes no counter for
      # `increase()` to find flat.
      - alert: FluxControllerDown
        expr: up{job="flux"} == 0
        for: 15m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "flux {{ $labels.controller }} is not answering its metrics port"
          description: >-
            Either the controller is down or its NodePort no longer matches. The ports are fixed in
            movies-gitops/flux-metrics/services.yaml and must agree with
            `fleet.prometheus.fluxTargets`; nothing checks that, and a disagreement looks exactly
            like this. Check `kubectl -n flux-system get pods` first -- if they are Running, it is
            the port.

      # RECONCILE DURATION, KEPT VISIBLE RATHER THAN ALERTED ON. This is a recording rule, not an
      # alert, because there is no threshold here backed by a measured baseline -- adding one would
      # be exactly the "coverage that isn't" this file warns against elsewhere. It exists because a
      # 2026-09-21 investigation into deploy timing had to reconstruct rollout duration by hand from
      # `kubectl` conditions and gitops commit timestamps (two samples, low confidence) when Flux's
      # own health-check wait (`wait: true` on every Kustomization) already times this on every
      # reconcile. Query `flux:kustomization_reconcile_duration_seconds:p95` by `name` instead of
      # redoing that archaeology. The same investigation found kustomize-controller retrying the web
      # Kustomizations against a 3-minute health-check timeout five times in 48h, self-healing each
      # time -- this is also how to tell whether that keeps happening before reaching for a real
      # alert threshold on it.
      - record: flux:kustomization_reconcile_duration_seconds:p95
        expr: >-
          histogram_quantile(0.95, sum(rate(gotk_reconcile_duration_seconds_bucket{kind="Kustomization"}[30m])) by (name, le))

  # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  # THE OBJECTS' OWN STATUS: IS EACH ONE READY, AND IS ANY OF THEM SUSPENDED.
  #
  # `flux:resource_info` is not a Flux metric. It is kube-state-metrics reading each Flux object's
  # `status.conditions[type=Ready].status` and `spec.suspend` through a custom-resource-state config
  # -- the shape Flux's own monitoring example recommends since the controllers dropped
  # `gotk_reconcile_condition` -- so it says what `flux get all -A` says, which the reconcile
  # counter above cannot: an object that reconciles on time and FAILS every time.
  #
  # ⚠️ IT EXISTS ONLY THROUGH THE GITOPS REPOSITORY. The config, its ConfigMap mount and the RBAC for
  # the flux API groups live in movies-gitops/kube-state-metrics/ (since 2026-09-24), which this
  # repository cannot change. Without them the two comparison rules below are silent by
  # construction, and `FluxObjectStateAbsent` fires to say so.
  #
  # NOT REPLAYED, and saying so is the point: there is no history of a series that has never been
  # published. The holds are argued from the configuration instead (see each rule), and the first
  # fortnight of real data is where they get checked.
  # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  - name: flux-objects
    rules:
      # ONE SERIES FAMILY PER KIND, UNIONED HERE. kube-state-metrics publishes a custom-resource
      # metric name from only ONE of the configured kinds when several share it: with all five
      # named `gotk_resource_info` it served 19 of the cluster's 22 objects -- no ImageRepository,
      # no ImageUpdateAutomation, no error logged -- and any pair of kinds served one (v2.14 to
      # v2.17 alike, measured against the live cluster 2026-09-24). So the gitops config names each
      # kind's metric `gotk_<kind>_info` and this puts them back together. `customresource_kind`
      # keeps the union free of duplicate label sets. The list is a contract with
      # movies-gitops/kube-state-metrics/flux-crs-config.yaml: a kind added there and not here is
      # published and never watched.
      - record: flux:resource_info
        expr: >-
          {__name__=~"gotk_(kustomization|gitrepository|imagerepository|imagepolicy|imageupdateautomation)_info"}

      # THIRTY MINUTES, from the Kustomizations' own settings: `interval: 10m` with `timeout: 10m`
      # on the web overlays and 3m elsewhere, and retries on the interval. One failed health check
      # therefore holds Ready=False for up to ~20 minutes before the next attempt can clear it,
      # and kustomize-controller did exactly that five times in 48h around 2026-09-21, healing
      # each time. Thirty minutes is past a single failed attempt and its retry; a third failure
      # in a row is not a slow rollout any more.
      - alert: FluxObjectNotReady
        expr: max by (customresource_kind, namespace, name) (flux:resource_info{ready="False"}) == 1
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "flux {{ $labels.customresource_kind }}/{{ $labels.name }} has been NotReady for 30 minutes"
          description: >-
            The object reconciles and FAILS -- the case `FluxReconciliationStopped` cannot see,
            because the reconcile counter keeps moving. For a Kustomization this means merged
            changes are not reaching the cluster: the pods keep serving the last image that did
            apply, with CI green. `flux get all -A` on monitoring-1 names the object and its
            message; `kubectl -n {{ $labels.namespace }} describe
            {{ $labels.customresource_kind | toLower }} {{ $labels.name }}` has the condition's
            full text. A health-check timeout usually means a pod that will not become Ready
            (see `K3sDeploymentReplicasMissing`); a build error means a bad manifest in
            movies-gitops.

      # AN HOUR, because a suspension is almost always DELIBERATE -- somebody ran `flux suspend`
      # to hold a change -- and the alert exists for the one that was never resumed. An hour is
      # long enough not to page the person holding it and short enough to land the same day.
      - alert: FluxObjectSuspended
        expr: max by (customresource_kind, namespace, name) (flux:resource_info{suspended="true"}) == 1
        for: 1h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "flux {{ $labels.customresource_kind }}/{{ $labels.name }} has been suspended for an hour"
          description: >-
            A suspended object reconciles NOTHING: its source can move, its images can be built
            and tagged, and the cluster does not change -- quietly, with every signal green,
            which is the 2026-09-03 failure by another door. If the hold is still wanted, silence
            this in Alertmanager with an expiry; if not, resume it (`flux resume kustomization
            {{ $labels.name }}` for a Kustomization; `flux resume --help` names the other kinds).

      - alert: FluxObjectStateAbsent
        expr: absent(flux:resource_info)
        # THIRTY MINUTES, matching every other `absent()` companion in this directory.
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "Flux object state is not published -- the kube-state-metrics gitops change is pending or broken"
          description: >-
            `FluxObjectNotReady` and `FluxObjectSuspended` cannot fire without
            `flux:resource_info` (kube-state-metrics' `gotk_<kind>_info` series), so a failing or suspended deploy path is unwatched. The series
            comes from movies-gitops/kube-state-metrics/: flux-crs-config.yaml (the
            custom-resource-state ConfigMap), the
            `--custom-resource-state-config-file=/etc/ksm/flux.yaml` flag and its volume mount in
            deployment.yaml, and list/watch on the flux API groups and on
            customresourcedefinitions in rbac.yaml. If one of those is missing on the cluster, the
            `kube-state-metrics` Kustomization has not applied it (`flux get kustomization
            kube-state-metrics`); if all are there, the pod's log names a reflector failing on
            permissions, or the config names a version the installed CRDs no longer serve
            (`kubectl get crd | grep fluxcd`). If `KubeStateMetricsSeriesAbsent` fires too, the
            exporter itself is the problem. IT DOES NOT MEAN FLUX IS HEALTHY.
